  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "The sides of a triangle have lengths of \\(15,\\) \\(20,\\) and \\(25.\\) Find the length of the shortest altitude.",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(6\\)",
    "B": "\\(12\\)",
    "C": "\\(12.5\\)",
    "D": "\\(13\\)",
    "E": "\\(15\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Every base-altitude pair gives the same triangle area. Since an altitude equals twice the area divided by its corresponding base, the shortest altitude belongs to the longest side.\n\nThe side lengths satisfy\n\\[\n15^2+20^2=225+400=625=25^2.\n\\]\nBy the converse of the Pythagorean theorem, the triangle is right, with perpendicular legs \\(15\\) and \\(20\\). Its area is\n\\[\nA=\\frac12(15)(20)=150.\n\\]\nUsing the longest side, \\(25\\), as the base gives the shortest altitude \\(h\\):\n\\[\n\\frac12(25)h=150,\n\\qquad h=\\frac{300}{25}=12.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{12}\\), choice B.",
